<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Documentation is enforced, not encouraged. GenerateDocumentationFile turns on CS1591
    ("missing XML comment for publicly visible type or member"), and TreatWarningsAsErrors
    above turns that into a build failure. An undocumented public API does not compile.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Vulnerability auditing. NuGetAuditMode=all covers the transitive graph, not just packages
    we reference by name — which is where most advisories actually live. NuGetAuditLevel=low
    means we hear about everything rather than only the alarming ones.

    Severity is deliberately split against TreatWarningsAsErrors:
      NU1901 low       -> warning
      NU1902 moderate  -> warning
      NU1903 high      -> ERROR
      NU1904 critical  -> ERROR
      NU1905 no source -> warning (audit source unreachable, e.g. offline build)

    Rationale: a new advisory can land against a package with no patched version yet. Making
    every severity fatal means an unrelated disclosure can block all work with no action
    available to unblock it, and the predictable response is that someone switches the whole
    audit off. Low and moderate stay loud but non-fatal; high and critical stop the build.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1905</WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    One version for everything this repository ships, declared here because a version set per
    project is a version that disagrees with itself. Semantic: major.minor.patch, and while the
    major is zero the minor is what moves on a breaking change.

    MSBuild's default when nothing is stated is 1.0.0, which would advertise every tool as a
    stable 1.0 of a protocol that expects breaking changes. A constant version also breaks
    reinstalling: `dotnet tool update` sees the same version it already has and does nothing,
    so a local rebuild appears to install and does not.

    Bump this in the same change that alters shipped behaviour, per the Versioning section of
    CLAUDE.md. A change that leaves it alone cannot be delivered by the documented upgrade
    path, and fails silently on the machine still running the old build.

    This is the *package* version and it is not the protocol's. SPEC.md carries its own, because
    it is what other implementations build against and a fix to the operator's CLI must not
    imply that the wire contract moved. The storage schema carries a third, in user_version.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VersionPrefix>0.44.2</VersionPrefix>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Authors>Arvind Knudsen</Authors>
    <Copyright>Copyright 2026 Arvind Knudsen</Copyright>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Shrp77/Parley</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Shrp77/Parley</PackageProjectUrl>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
